.\"
.TH HVI 1 "2026-07-06" "HVI 2.1.1" "CP/M User Commands"
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH NAME
.\"
hvi \- VI-compatible text editor for CP/M
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\"
.B HVI
.RI [ \-d ]
.RI [ filename ]
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH DESCRIPTION
.\"
.B HVI
is a lightweight VI-compatible text editor for CP/M 2.2 and CP/M 3.0.
It uses a gap buffer for efficient editing and ANSI/VT100 escape sequences
for terminal control.
.PP
If
.I filename
is given and the file exists, it is loaded for editing.
If the file does not exist, an empty buffer is opened and the file is created
on the first write.
If no filename is given, HVI starts with an unnamed empty buffer.
.PP
HVI supports three editing modes: normal mode (the default), insert mode,
and command-line (ex) mode.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH OPTIONS
.\"
.TP
.B \-d
Enable debug output.
Diagnostic messages (key codes, mode changes, buffer allocation, terminal
size negotiation) are written to the console via
.IR stderr .
On CP/M,
.I stderr
maps to CON: and cannot be redirected to a file; messages appear on screen
interleaved with the editor display and will disrupt it.
Debug mode is most useful for diagnosing startup and initialization problems
(before full-screen editing begins), or when running under a Unix
cross-compile environment where stderr redirection is available:
.PP
.RS
.B hvi \-d myfile.txt 2>debug.txt
.RE
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH MODES
.\"
.SS Normal Mode
.PP
The default mode on startup.
Keystrokes are interpreted as cursor-movement or editing commands.
.SS Insert Mode
.PP
Entered from normal mode via
.BR i ,
.BR a ,
.BR I ,
.BR A ,
.BR o ,
.BR O ,
.BR s ,
or
.BR S .
All printable characters are inserted into the buffer.
Press
.B ESC
to return to normal mode.
.SS Command-Line (Ex) Mode
.PP
Entered from normal mode by pressing
.BR : .
A command prompt appears on the status line.
Press
.B Enter
to execute or
.B ESC
to cancel.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH COMMANDS
.\"
.SS Count Prefix
Most normal-mode commands accept a numeric count prefix that repeats the
command.
For example,
.B 5j
moves down five lines and
.B 3dd
deletes three lines.
.\"
.SS Cursor Movement
.TP
.BR "h" " / left-arrow"
Move left one character.
.TP
.BR "l" " / right-arrow"
Move right one character.
.TP
.BR "j" " / down-arrow"
Move down one line.
.TP
.BR "k" " / up-arrow"
Move up one line.
.TP
.B Enter
Move to the first non-blank character of the next line.
Accepts a count prefix to skip multiple lines.
.TP
.B w
Move forward to the start of the next word.
.TP
.B b
Move backward to the start of the previous word.
.TP
.B e
Move forward to the end of the current or next word.
.TP
.B 0
Move to the beginning of the line.
.TP
.B ^
Move to the first non-blank character of the line.
.TP
.B $
Move to the end of the line.
.TP
.BI G
Go to the last line of the file.
With a count prefix
.RI ( nG ),
go to line
.IR n .
.TP
.B gg
Go to the first line of the file.
With a count prefix
.RI ( ngg ),
go to line
.IR n .
.TP
.B Ctrl-F
Scroll forward one page.
The cursor is placed at the middle row of the new page on the first
non-blank character.
If the end of the file is already visible, this command is a no-op.
.TP
.B Ctrl-B
Scroll backward one page.
The cursor is placed at the middle row of the new page on the first
non-blank character.
If the beginning of the file is already displayed, this command is a no-op.
.TP
.B Ctrl-D
Scroll forward half a page.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Scroll backward half a page.
.\"
.SS Character Search (Current Line)
.TP
.BI f c
Move forward to the next occurrence of character
.I c
on the current line.
.TP
.BI F c
Move backward to the previous occurrence of character
.I c
on the current line.
.TP
.B ;
Repeat the last
.B f
or
.B F
in the same direction.
.TP
.B ,
Repeat the last
.B f
or
.B F
in the opposite direction.
.\"
.SS Inserting Text
.TP
.B i
Insert before the cursor.
.TP
.B a
Append after the cursor.
.TP
.B I
Insert at the first non-blank character of the line.
.TP
.B A
Append at the end of the line.
.TP
.B o
Open a new line below the current line and enter insert mode.
.TP
.B O
Open a new line above the current line and enter insert mode.
.TP
.B s
Delete
.I n
characters and enter insert mode (substitute).
.TP
.B S
Delete the content of the current line and enter insert mode.
.\"
.SS Deleting Text
.TP
.B x
Delete the character under the cursor.
.TP
.B X
Delete the character before the cursor.
.TP
.B D
Delete from the cursor to the end of the line.
.TP
.B dd
Delete the current line.
.TP
.B dw
Delete from the cursor to the start of the next word.
.TP
.B db
Delete from the cursor to the start of the previous word.
.TP
.B d$
Delete from the cursor to the end of the line.
.TP
.B d0
Delete from the cursor to the beginning of the line.
.TP
.B dG
Delete from the current line to the end of the file.
.\"
.SS Changing Text
.TP
.B r
Replace the character under the cursor with the next key typed.
.TP
.B ~
Toggle the case of the character under the cursor and advance.
.TP
.B C
Change from the cursor to the end of the line (delete then enter insert mode).
.TP
.B cc
Change the current line.
.TP
.B cw
Change from the cursor to the start of the next word.
.TP
.B c$
Change from the cursor to the end of the line.
.TP
.B J
Join the line below to the current line, separated by a space.
.\"
.SS Yank and Put
.TP
.B yy
Yank (copy) the current line into the yank buffer.
.TP
.B Y
Yank the current line (same as
.BR yy ).
.TP
.B yw
Yank from the cursor to the start of the next word.
.TP
.B y$
Yank from the cursor to the end of the line.
.TP
.B p
Put (paste) the yank buffer after the cursor.
For linewise yanks, the text is inserted below the current line.
.TP
.B P
Put the yank buffer before the cursor.
For linewise yanks, the text is inserted above the current line.
.\"
.SS Search
.TP
.BI / pattern
Search forward for
.IR pattern .
.TP
.BI ? pattern
Search backward for
.IR pattern .
.TP
.B n
Repeat the last search in the same direction.
.TP
.B N
Repeat the last search in the opposite direction.
.PP
Patterns are plain-text strings (no regular expressions).
.\"
.SS Miscellaneous Normal-Mode Commands
.TP
.B .
Repeat the last change command, optionally with a new count prefix.
.TP
.B u
Undo the last change.
Only one level of undo is maintained.
.TP
.B :
Enter ex command-line mode.
.TP
.B Ctrl-L
Redraw the screen.
.\"
.SS Insert Mode Keys
.TP
.I character
Insert the character at the cursor.
.TP
.B Enter
Insert a newline.
.TP
.BR Backspace " / " Ctrl-H
Delete the previous character.
.TP
.B Ctrl-W
Delete the previous word.
.TP
.B Ctrl-U
Delete to the start of the current line.
.TP
.B up-arrow / down-arrow / left-arrow / right-arrow
Move the cursor without leaving insert mode.
.TP
.B ESC
Return to normal mode.
.\"
.SS Ex Commands
.TP
.B :w
Write (save) the current buffer to its file.
.TP
.BI :w " filename"
Write the buffer to
.IR filename .
.TP
.B :q
Quit.
Fails if the buffer has unsaved changes; use
.B :q!
to override.
.TP
.B :q!
Quit without saving.
.TP
.B :wq
Write the buffer and quit.
.TP
.B :wq!
Write the buffer and quit, even if the file is read-only.
.TP
.B :x
Write the buffer if it has been modified, then quit.
.TP
.B :x!
Write the buffer and quit (force).
.TP
.BI :e " filename"
Abandon the current buffer and begin editing
.IR filename .
Fails if the buffer has unsaved changes; use
.B :e!
to override.
.TP
.BI :e! " filename"
Abandon the current buffer (discarding any unsaved changes) and begin editing
.IR filename .
.TP
.BI :r " filename"
Read
.I filename
and insert its contents after the current line.
.TP
.BI : n
Go to line number
.IR n .
.TP
.B :$
Go to the last line.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH STATUS LINE
.\"
The bottom row of the screen is the status line.
During normal editing it displays:
.PP
.RS
.RI \(dq filename \(dq\ [+]\ L current / total
.RE
.PP
.I filename
is the name of the file being edited, or
.B [No Name]
if no file has been specified.
The
.B [+]
flag appears when the buffer contains unsaved changes.
.I current
is the current line number (1-based) and
.I total
is the total number of lines in the loaded buffer.
.PP
When a file is too large to fit entirely in memory, a
.B +
is appended to the total:
.PP
.RS
.RI \(dq filename \(dq\ L42/300+
.RE
.PP
The
.B +
indicates that additional content exists in the file beyond what is
currently loaded.
.PP
Whenever HVI reads the next chunk of a large file from disk, it briefly
displays
.B [Loading...]
on the status line so that the user knows disk I/O is in progress.
The indicator is replaced by the normal status display as soon as the
screen is refreshed after the load completes.
.PP
While in insert mode the status line shows
.BR "-- INSERT --" .
Error and informational messages (such as search results and undo confirmations)
are displayed on the status line and cleared on the next keypress.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH LARGE FILE SUPPORT
.\"
HVI can open and edit files larger than available RAM.
At startup it allocates the largest single contiguous block the system can
provide (typically up to about 28 KB on a CP/M system with 64 KB RAM) and
loads as much of the file as fits.
.PP
As you scroll forward, HVI automatically loads the next chunk of the file
into the buffer.
When the buffer is full, an equal number of bytes are discarded from the
beginning of the buffer to make room.
The in-memory content is therefore a
.I sliding window
over the file.
The status line indicates when unloaded content remains with a
.B +
after the line count.
.PP
On every
.B :w
save the complete file is reconstructed:
any bytes before the current window are copied from the original file,
the in-memory content is written (with
.B CR+LF
line endings), and the unloaded tail is appended.
When saving back to the same file, an intermediate temporary file
.RB ( HVITMP.TMP )
is used to avoid reading and writing the same file simultaneously.
.PP
Edits are restricted to the portion of the file currently in the buffer.
Content that has scrolled off the front or not yet been loaded from the
tail is preserved unchanged on save.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH TERMINAL REQUIREMENTS
.\"
HVI requires an ANSI/VT100-compatible terminal.
It defaults to 80 columns \(mu 24 rows on startup.
.PP
Compatible terminals include VT100, VT220, xterm, ANSI.SYS, and most
modern terminal emulators connected via a serial port.
.PP
At startup HVI queries the terminal for its actual dimensions by sending
.B ESC[999;999H
followed by
.B ESC[6n
(ANSI cursor-position report).
The response is read byte-by-byte via BIOS CONIN, bypassing BDOS canonical
buffering.
If the terminal does not respond within the polling timeout, HVI silently
falls back to the compiled-in defaults
.RB ( DEF_COLS =80,
.BR DEF_ROWS =24).
No recompilation flag is required; the query is always active.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH FILE FORMAT
.\"
HVI reads files in binary mode, stripping bare
.B CR
(0x0D) characters on load so that both Unix (LF) and CP/M (CR+LF) files
are handled transparently.
.PP
On save, each
.B LF
is written as
.B CR+LF
per CP/M convention, and the file is terminated with
.B Ctrl-Z
(0x1A) per CP/M file format rules.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH PERFORMANCE
.\"
HVI minimises terminal output at every keypress.
Updates are sized to the smallest region that needs to change:
.TP
.B Cursor move only
.BR h ,
.BR l ,
.BR 0 ,
.BR ^ ,
.BR $ ,
.BR f ,
.BR F ,
.BR ; ,
.B ,
\(em text is unchanged; only the terminal cursor is repositioned.
.TP
.B Terminal scroll + one row (or cursor only)
.BR j ,
.BR k ,
Enter,
.BR w ,
.BR b ,
.BR e ,
.BR / ,
.BR ? ,
.BR n ,
.BR N ,
.BR gg ,
.I nG
\(em when the viewport shifts by exactly one visual row, the terminal
hardware scroll is used and a single new row is drawn (~53 bytes).
When no scroll is needed only the cursor is repositioned.
The status bar is not updated on these keystrokes.
.TP
.B Single visual row
.B r
\(em character count unchanged; only the row containing the cursor is redrawn.
.TP
.B Current logical line
.BR x ,
.BR X ,
.BR D ,
.BR ~ ,
.BR s ,
.BR S ,
.B C
\(em change is confined to the current line; only that line's visual rows are redrawn.
.TP
.B Cursor row to bottom
.BR J ,
.BR o ,
.BR O ,
.BR p ,
.BR P ,
.BR u ,
.BR dw ,
.BR dd ,
.BR cw ,
Enter in insert mode
\(em content above the cursor is unchanged; only rows from the cursor to
the bottom of the text area are redrawn.
On a 24-row terminal with the cursor in the middle this sends roughly
half the bytes of a full refresh (~600 vs ~1200 bytes at 9600 baud).
.TP
.B Full screen
.BR G ,
.BR Ctrl-F ,
.BR Ctrl-B ,
.BR Ctrl-D ,
.BR Ctrl-U ,
and ex commands
\(em viewport jump or file content change requires a complete redraw.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH LIMITATIONS
.\"
.IP \(bu 2
Single-level undo only.
The
.B u
command undoes the most recent change; there is no redo.
.IP \(bu 2
Edits to large files are restricted to the in-memory window.
See
.B LARGE FILE SUPPORT
above.
.IP \(bu 2
Pattern matching for
.B /
and
.B ?
is plain text only; regular expressions are not supported.
.IP \(bu 2
No visual or block selection mode.
.IP \(bu 2
No macro recording or playback.
.IP \(bu 2
No window splitting or multiple buffers.
.\"
.\" ----------------------------------------------------------------
.SH FILES
.\"
.TP
.B HVITMP.TMP
Temporary file created in the current directory during a save when the
source and destination file are the same and a tail exists.
Removed automatically after a successful save.
